---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy | Liberating Scripture Collective"
  description="How the Liberating Scripture Collective website and the LIT Bible apps handle your data: what little we collect, the services we rely on, and what we never do."
  canonical="https://liberatingscripture.org/privacy/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script
      type="application/ld+json"
      is:inline
      set:html={JSON.stringify({
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "WebPage",
        "@id": "https://liberatingscripture.org/privacy/#webpage",
        url: "https://liberatingscripture.org/privacy/",
        name: "Privacy Policy | Liberating Scripture Collective",
        dateModified: "2026-07-21",
        isPartOf: { "@id": "https://liberatingscripture.org/#website" },
      })}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="privacy-page">
    <header class="privacy-hero">
      <div class="content">
        <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
        <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          We built this site to hold as little of your data as possible, and the
          LIT Bible apps on the same principle: your reading life is your own.
          The most trustworthy thing we can do with information about you is not
          collect it in the first place.
        </p>
        <p class="privacy-effective">
          Effective date: <time datetime="2026-07-21">July 21, 2026</time>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="privacy-body content">
      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-scope">
        <h2 id="privacy-scope">Scope</h2>
        <p>
          This policy covers the Liberating Scripture Collective website at
          liberatingscripture.org and the companion LIT Bible iOS and Android
          apps. Sections labeled "(app)" describe how the apps handle data.
          Where a detail is platform-specific (for example, iCloud sync on iOS
          or automatic Google Drive backup on Android), it is labeled
          <em>On iOS</em> or <em>On Android</em>; details that apply to both are
          labeled <em>On both platforms</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The LIT Bible also has its own website at litbible.net. That site is
          covered by the
          <a href="https://litbible.net/privacy/">litbible.net privacy policy</a>,
          which is also the policy of record referenced by the App Store and
          Google Play listings. The app sections below are kept identical to it;
          if the two ever disagree, the litbible.net version governs the apps.
          Nothing you do on this site is shared with the apps, and nothing from
          them flows back here.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-collect">
        <h2 id="privacy-collect">What we collect</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Website:</strong> The site collects personal information only
          when you actively send it to us:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Mailing list:</strong> If you subscribe using the form in
            the footer, your email address is stored by Brevo, which manages
            the mailing list solely to send updates about the Collective's
            work. This is a separate list from the LIT Bible's own translation
            updates. You can unsubscribe anytime using the links in those
            emails, or on our <a href="/unsubscribe/">unsubscribe page</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Contact form:</strong> Your name, email address, and
            message are delivered directly to our inbox by Cloudflare, the
            infrastructure this site runs behind. No separate form-processing
            service handles them. We use what you send only to respond to you;
            it is never sold or shared.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Donations:</strong> Gifts on the
            <a href="/support/">support page</a> are processed by
            <a href="https://www.givelively.org/">Give Lively</a>, a nonprofit
            fundraising platform, and its payment processors, subject to
            <a href="https://www.givelively.org/privacy">Give Lively's privacy policy</a>.
            Your payment details go to them, not to us. This site never sees
            or stores them. We receive what we need to acknowledge your gift
            (like your name and email), and we don't sell or share donor
            information.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The contact and subscribe forms are protected from spam by Cloudflare
          Turnstile, a privacy-preserving alternative to CAPTCHAs that does not
          track you across sites.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Cookies:</strong> The site sets one cookie of its own, a small
          flag (<code>lsc_apps_launch_v1</code>) that remembers you have seen the
          one-time announcement about the LIT Bible apps, so it doesn't reappear.
          It contains no personal data and expires after 30 days. The same
          announcement counts pageviews within a single browsing session, using
          your browser's session storage (<code>lsc_pv</code>), so it can wait
          until your second page rather than interrupting you on arrival. That
          count never leaves your device and is discarded when you close the tab.
        </p>
        <p>
          The site also uses Cloudflare Web Analytics, which is
          privacy-preserving by design: it sets no cookies, does not track
          individuals across sites or sessions, and does not build behavioral
          profiles. We see only aggregated numbers like page views and general
          geographic region, none of which can identify you.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>App (on both platforms):</strong> The app does not collect your
          name, email address, device identifiers, or identifying information.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-not">
        <h2 id="privacy-not">What we don't do</h2>
        <p>
          There are no accounts to create, no advertising networks, and no
          cross-site tracking. The single cookie we set is the announcement flag
          described above — it holds no personal data — and our only measurement
          is the cookie-free, aggregate analytics. The one exception is the
          donation widget on the
          <a href="/support/">support page</a>: it is operated by
          <a href="https://www.givelively.org/">Give Lively</a>, runs on their
          systems, and may set its own cookies or storage when you use it to
          give (see
          <a href="https://www.givelively.org/privacy">Give Lively's privacy policy</a>).
          Apart from that, you can read every page without telling us anything
          about yourself.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-reading-data">
        <h2 id="privacy-reading-data">Your reading data (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> Everything you create in the app
          (highlights, notes, and your reading position) is stored privately on
          your device. The LIT Bible team cannot access this data.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>On iOS:</strong> If you have iCloud enabled for LIT Bible,
          this data is also synced to your personal iCloud account via Apple's
          CloudKit. Sync happens automatically in the background, the same way
          Apple Notes and Reminders work. Your data is stored in your own
          private iCloud database; the LIT Bible team has no access to it there
          either. You can turn off iCloud sync for LIT Bible at any time in iOS
          Settings → [Your Name] → iCloud → Apps Using iCloud. App preferences
          like text size and display settings are stored in your device's
          standard preferences storage and are not synced to iCloud.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>On Android:</strong> If your device has Backup enabled, a copy
          of your highlights and notes is included in your device's automatic
          backup to your own Google Drive (via Android Auto Backup), so they can
          restore on a new device. This backup lives in your personal Google
          account; the LIT Bible team has no access to it there either. You can
          turn it off at any time in your device's settings under Settings →
          Google → Backup. Your reading position and your app preferences (like
          text size and display settings) are stored on your device but are not
          included in this backup; only your highlights and notes are.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-content-updates">
        <h2 id="privacy-content-updates">Content updates (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> The app periodically checks
          litbible.net servers for Bible translation text updates. During this
          check, your device downloads changed content with no information about
          you, your device, or reading activity transmitted to the servers. The
          process compares file versions and downloads updated text only.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-widget">
        <h2 id="privacy-widget">The home screen widget (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> The LIT Bible widget displays
          lectionary readings and liturgical calendar information. All widget
          data is computed on your device from information bundled with the app.
          The widget makes no network requests.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-sharing">
        <h2 id="privacy-sharing">Sharing (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> When you select text and choose
          Share, your device's standard share sheet opens. The selected passage
          text is passed to whatever app you choose to share it with. The LIT
          Bible team does not log what you share, where you share it, or
          frequency.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-third-party">
        <h2 id="privacy-third-party">Third-party software (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On iOS,</strong> the app uses one third-party software
          library: <strong>GRDB</strong>, an open-source database library that
          stores scripture content locally on your device. It makes no network
          connections of its own. iCloud sync uses Apple's CloudKit framework,
          which is part of the iOS operating system and subject to Apple's
          privacy policy.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>On Android,</strong> the app uses two third-party software
          libraries: <strong>Room</strong> (an open-source database library from
          Google that stores scripture content locally on your device using the
          device's built-in SQLite), and <strong>OkHttp</strong> (an open-source
          HTTP library used only for the content-update downloads described
          above, fetching translation text from litbible.net). Automatic backup
          of your highlights and notes uses Android Auto Backup, which is part
          of the Android operating system and subject to Google's privacy
          policy.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-external-links">
        <h2 id="privacy-external-links">External links</h2>
        <p>
          The website and app include links to external services. Following
          these links subjects you to those third parties' privacy policies. We
          do not track whether you visit these pages or take actions on them.
        </p>
        <p>External services linked include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Give Lively</strong>, for contributions to the Liberating
            Scripture Collective and the LIT Bible
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Apple Podcasts</strong>, for the Found in Translation
            podcast
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Spotify</strong>, for the Found in Translation podcast
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>YouTube</strong>, for the Found in Translation podcast
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The App Store and Google Play</strong>, for the LIT Bible
            apps
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>CreativeCommons.org</strong>, to display the full license
            text
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>litbible.net</strong>, the LIT Bible's own website
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-children">
        <h2 id="privacy-children">Children</h2>
        <p>
          This website and the LIT Bible app do not knowingly collect personal
          information from children. The site and app contain no advertising and
          no social features. Any personal data the site receives, such as a
          newsletter subscription or a message sent through the contact form,
          requires you to actively submit it and is never required to access
          content.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-hosting">
        <h2 id="privacy-hosting">Hosting and infrastructure</h2>
        <p>
          The site is hosted on GitHub Pages and served through Cloudflare.
          Like nearly all web infrastructure, both may log basic request data
          (such as IP addresses) at the network level for security and
          performance, subject to
          <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub's privacy statement</a>
          and
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.
          We don't have access to, and don't build anything on top of, those
          logs.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-changes">
        <h2 id="privacy-changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
          If the site or app gains features that touch your data, such as an
          embedded podcast player, we'll describe them here before they matter
          and update the effective date above. Changes that materially affect
          how app data is handled are also noted in the app's Translation
          Updates section.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-contact">
        <h2 id="privacy-contact">Questions</h2>
        <p>
          If anything here is unclear, or you want to know more about how
          your information is handled, <a href="/contact/">contact us</a>.
          A real person reads every message.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .privacy-hero {
    background: var(--surface);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem) 0;
    position: relative;
  }

  .privacy-hero::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    top: 0; left: 0; right: 0;
    height: 5px;
    background: var(--green);
  }

  .privacy-hero .content {
    max-width: 64ch;
  }

  .privacy-hero .eyebrow { color: var(--green-text); }

  .privacy-hero h1 {
    font-family: "Crimson Text", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 4.5vw, 3.5rem);
    color: var(--text-strong);
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .privacy-effective {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
  }

  .privacy-body {
    max-width: 64ch;
    padding: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem) 0 clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }

  .privacy-body section + section {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  .privacy-body h2 {
    font-family: "Crimson Text", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--text-strong);
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .privacy-body p,
  .privacy-body ul {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .privacy-body ul {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }

  .privacy-body li {
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  .privacy-body a {
    color: var(--green-text);
  }

  /* Cookie / storage key names */
  .privacy-body code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 0.88em;
    background: var(--surface-input);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Platform labels in the Scope section */
  .privacy-body em {
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }
</style>
